---
import LandingLayout from '../layouts/LandingLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/seo/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CtaBox from '../components/seo/CtaBox.astro';
import FaqAccordion from '../components/seo/FaqAccordion.astro';
import { breadcrumbList, faqPage, organization, webSite } from '../lib/schema';

const path = '/best-bookmark-manager';
const title = 'Best Bookmark Managers in 2027: 7 Compared, Matrix Inside';
const description =
  'A transparent comparison of 7 bookmark managers in 2027, covering retrieval, filing effort, multimedia capture, portability, platform support, and price.';
const canonical = 'https://www.marqly.com/best-bookmark-manager';

const crumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
  { name: 'The 2027 Bookmark Manager Index', path },
];

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What is the best bookmark manager overall in 2027?',
    a: 'There is no universal winner. Marqly is the strongest fit when semantic search and AI-assisted capture matter most. Raindrop.io is better for visual, nested collections, broad native-app coverage, and an unlimited free tier. Readwise Reader is built for intensive reading and highlight workflows. The right choice depends on which workflow you need to improve.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why is semantic search so critical for bookmark managers in 2027?',
    a: 'Traditional bookmark managers rely on exact keyword and tag matching. If you save an article titled "PostgreSQL MVCC Internals" and months later search for "database vacuum dead tuples", keyword search returns zero results. Semantic search indexes what a page is about rather than only the strings in it, so you can locate a saved link or highlighted snippet by describing it from memory. Note that it is a paid feature in every tool that offers it, including Marqly, where it is part of Pro.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I migrate my bookmarks from Raindrop.io without losing tags or folders?',
    a: 'Export your Raindrop library as an HTML file from Settings → Backups and import it into the new tool. Marqly reads Raindrop\'s HTML export (not its JSON export), and titles, URLs, descriptions and tags carry across. Two limits to plan around rather than discover later: folder structure flattens to at most two board levels, and imported items take the import date rather than their original save date. Auto-tagging an import is a Pro feature, so a free import lands with your existing tags intact and no new ones. You can inspect the file first with the free Raindrop Export Analyzer.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I capture YouTube video transcripts and notes in a bookmark manager?',
    a: 'Most bookmark managers save only the YouTube URL and thumbnail. Marqly adds an AI card to the YouTube watch page itself with three tabs: a streaming summary with key sections, a playback-synced transcript you can copy in one click, and a chat tab that answers questions from that transcript on Pro. Saving the video from YouTube\'s own action row keeps the transcript attached to the bookmark, so the video stays searchable by what was said in it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is a free bookmark manager sufficient, or do I need a paid plan?',
    a: 'A free plan is often enough for basic saving and manual organization, and Raindrop.io is unlimited on free. Marqly Free stores up to 100 bookmarks with whole-library keyword search and includes boards, six-colour highlighting and public board sharing. Marqly\'s AI summaries, semantic search, AI Organizer and YouTube chat require Pro, which is $72 per year or $9 monthly, with a standing $49 first-year offer and $48 for verified students.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What happens if I decide to switch tools later (data portability)?',
    a: 'Never choose a bookmark tool you cannot leave. Marqly has a CSV export button (sidebar or profile menu → Export): the free plan exports the 100 most recent bookmarks, and Pro exports the whole library, with an option to limit the file to items added since your last export. That CSV carries URL, title, description and tags. Highlights, notes and boards are not in the CSV — for a complete copy of your account including those, email support@marqly.com. Test any export on day one rather than at the moment you need it; that habit is the real lesson of the Pocket and Omnivore shutdowns.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the best bookmarking tool in 2027?',
    a: '"Bookmarking tool" covers three different shapes, and the best one depends on which you mean. For an online tool with nothing to install, Marqly and Raindrop.io both run entirely in a browser alongside their apps. For organising a large existing library, Raindrop.io has the strongest free tier and the best nested collections. For finding something again from a half-memory, Marqly is the strongest because semantic search is its core design rather than an add-on. For owning your data outright, Linkwarden or Karakeep self-hosted. There is no single winner across all four jobs.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the best online bookmark manager, with nothing to install?',
    a: 'Marqly and Raindrop.io are the two strongest browser-first options. Both keep the library in the cloud and work fully from a web app, so a new machine costs nothing to set up; both also ship extensions and mobile apps if you later want faster capture. Marqly adds public boards that open as a plain web page with no signup for the viewer, which is free on every plan. The trade-off of any online manager is that your access depends on the vendor, which is why the durability criteria below matter.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Will my bookmark manager still exist in a few years?',
    a: 'It is a fair question now, because two widely recommended tools disappeared within eight months: Omnivore shut down in November 2024 after its team was acqui-hired, and Mozilla shut Pocket down on July 8, 2025 with its export window closing on November 12, 2025. Three things predict survival — an export path you have actually tested, a revenue model that is not free-with-no-revenue, and open-source code that survives the company. Wallabag (MIT), Linkwarden (AGPL-3.0) and Karakeep score best on the third; every hosted tool, Marqly included, is weaker there.',
  },
];


const jsonLd = [
  organization(),
  webSite(),
  breadcrumbList(crumbs),
  faqPage(faqs),
];
---

<LandingLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <article class="index-page">
    <div class="container">
      <Breadcrumbs items={crumbs} />

      <header class="hero-header">
        <div class="badge-pill">2027 Editorial Comparison</div>
        <h1 class="page-title">Best Bookmark Managers in 2027</h1>
        <p class="hero-sub">
          {description}
        </p>
        <div class="meta-bar">
          <span><strong>Published:</strong> September 2026</span>
          <span class="sep">•</span>
          <span><strong>Updated:</strong> October 29, 2026</span>
          <span class="sep">•</span>
          <span><strong>Scope:</strong> 7 tools across 7 decision criteria</span>
        </div>
      </header>

      <!-- Table of Contents -->
      <nav class="toc-card" aria-label="Table of Contents">
        <div class="toc-title">Table of Contents & Quick Navigation</div>
        <ol class="toc-grid">
          <li><a href="#methodology">1. How this comparison works</a></li>
          <li><a href="#durability">2. What changed for 2027: durability</a></li>
          <li><a href="#comparative-matrix">3. 2027 feature matrix</a></li>
          <li><a href="#retrieval-battery">4. Retrieval & semantic search</a></li>
          <li><a href="#filing-latency">5. Filing effort</a></li>
          <li><a href="#multimedia">6. Multimedia & video capture</a></li>
          <li><a href="#portability">7. Portability & export fidelity</a></li>
          <li><a href="#by-audience">8. Choosing by audience</a></li>
          <li><a href="#tool-profiles">9. Tool profiles</a></li>
          <li><a href="#faq">10. Frequently Asked Questions</a></li>
        </ol>
      </nav>

      <!-- Section 1: Methodology -->
      <section id="methodology" class="index-section">
        <h2>How this comparison works</h2>
        <p>
          Bookmark managers entered a fundamental paradigm shift across 2025 and 2026, and 2027 is the first year the shift is settled rather than in progress. For twenty years, personal web curation relied on manual folders and tags. Users spent seconds on every save deciding taxonomy, only to abandon organizational hygiene when collections grew past 500 links. In practice, traditional bookmark folders become "write-only" link cemeteries. Two shutdowns then removed the default answers — Omnivore in November 2024 and Pocket in July 2025 — which is why durability is now a seventh criterion rather than an afterthought.
        </p>
        <p>
          This index compares seven established bookmark and read-it-later tools using seven practical decision criteria: retrieval, filing effort, multimedia capture, data portability, reading and archive features, platform/privacy trade-offs, and durability. The matrix is a feature comparison based on documented product capabilities and our maintained competitor records, last re-verified in October 2026. It is <strong>not a laboratory performance test</strong>, so we do not publish synthetic precision scores or pretend one tool wins every workflow.
        </p>

        <div class="framework-grid">
          <div class="framework-card">
            <h3>1. Retrieval</h3>
            <p>Whether search is limited to keywords and tags or can retrieve by conceptual meaning.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="framework-card">
            <h3>2. Filing effort</h3>
            <p>How much folder selection, tagging, and cleanup a user must do after saving.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="framework-card">
            <h3>3. Multimedia Capture</h3>
            <p>Support for video transcripts, YouTube notes, PDFs, and durable page capture.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="framework-card">
            <h3>4. Data Portability</h3>
            <p>What the export actually contains, and how clearly users can move their library elsewhere.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="framework-card">
            <h3>5. Reading & Archive</h3>
            <p>Reader mode, highlights, offline access, PDF support, and web archives.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="framework-card">
            <h3>6. Privacy & Sync</h3>
            <p>Native-app coverage, hosting model, collaboration, and meaningful privacy controls.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="framework-card">
            <h3>7. Durability</h3>
            <p>Whether the tool and your library survive the vendor: licence, revenue model, tested export path.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 2: Durability (new for the 2027 edition) -->
      <section id="durability" class="index-section">
        <h2>What changed for 2027: durability became a criterion</h2>
        <p>
          Nobody evaluating bookmark managers in 2024 was asking whether the product would still exist. In 2027 that question has an evidence base. <strong>Omnivore shut down in November 2024</strong> after its team was acqui-hired by ElevenLabs; users had until November 30, 2024 to export before deletion. Omnivore had been completely free with no paid plan at any point — a model that is exactly what made the shutdown possible. <strong>Mozilla shut Pocket down on July 8, 2025</strong>, disabled export access and the API on November 12, 2025, and states that remaining data was queued for permanent deletion from that date. Premium subscribers received prorated refunds; unexported libraries did not survive.
        </p>
        <p>
          Three properties predict whether your library outlives the vendor, and all three are checkable in ten minutes:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>An export path you have actually tested.</strong> Not a documented feature — a file you produced and reopened. The detail that matters is what the export <em>contains</em>: Marqly's CSV carries URL, title, description and tags, while highlights, notes and boards require a support request for a complete account copy. A tool whose export omits half your work is a partial exit.</li>
          <li><strong>A revenue model that is not free-with-no-revenue.</strong> Paid plans with real subscribers, and long-lived open source projects with a hosted option funding development, both outlast a free product with no income.</li>
          <li><strong>An open-source licence.</strong> Wallabag (MIT, maintained continuously since 2013), Linkwarden (AGPL-3.0, all features included when self-hosted) and Karakeep all survive their company as forks. Proprietary tools leave you with whatever you exported.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          This is the reason we publish the measured <a href="/research/bookmark-import-fidelity">import-fidelity study</a> instead of asserting fidelity: the study records which real export files a real importer can and cannot read, including the finding that Pocket's HTML preview file does not parse and that the <code>list.csv</code> inside the archive is what works.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 2: Comparative Matrix -->
      <section id="comparative-matrix" class="index-section">
        <h2>2027 feature matrix</h2>
        <p>
          Start with the workflow you care about. “Best” here means best fit for that job, not the highest invented score.
        </p>

        <div class="table-wrapper" style="overflow-x: auto;">
          <table class="comparison-table benchmark-matrix">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col">Tool</th>
                <th scope="col">Search model</th>
                <th scope="col">Filing model</th>
                <th scope="col">Video support</th>
                <th scope="col">Export / ownership</th>
                <th scope="col">Best Use Case</th>
                <th scope="col">Pricing Model</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr class="highlight-row">
                <td>
                  <strong>Marqly</strong>
                  <span class="sub-badge">Best for AI retrieval</span>
                </td>
                <td>Semantic + full-text on Pro; keyword on Free</td>
                <td>AI auto-tagging and summaries on Pro</td>
                <td>Transcript, summary, and chat on Pro</td>
                <td>HTML export; browser-side PDF capture</td>
                <td>AI semantic retrieval & YouTube study</td>
                <td>Free; Pro $72/year or $9/month ($49 first year)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>
                  <strong>Raindrop.io</strong>
                </td>
                <td>Keyword, tag, and full-text features</td>
                <td>Manual collections and tags; Stella suggestions</td>
                <td>Link metadata and preview</td>
                <td>HTML, CSV, and backups</td>
                <td>Visual moodboards & nested folders</td>
                <td>Free; Pro $28/year or $3.25/month</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>
                  <strong>Readwise Reader</strong>
                </td>
                <td>Full-text plus document AI tools</td>
                <td>Inbox and reading workflow</td>
                <td>Transcript and document reading tools</td>
                <td>PKM exports and integrations</td>
                <td>EPUB/PDF deep reading & PKM sync</td>
                <td>Paid subscription</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>
                  <strong>Karakeep</strong>
                </td>
                <td>Full-text plus configurable AI</td>
                <td>Tags and lists; optional AI</td>
                <td>Metadata and archive capture</td>
                <td>Self-hosted, open-source database</td>
                <td>Self-hosted local AI inference</td>
                <td>Open Source (Free)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>
                  <strong>Linkwarden</strong>
                </td>
                <td>Full-text search</td>
                <td>Collections and tags</td>
                <td>Archive snapshots</td>
                <td>Self-hosted option and exports</td>
                <td>Team link archiving & PDF snapshots</td>
                <td>Open source or hosted subscription</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>
                  <strong>Anybox</strong>
                </td>
                <td>Keyword and tag search</td>
                <td>Native Apple capture workflow</td>
                <td>Link metadata</td>
                <td>Local-first Apple workflow and exports</td>
                <td>macOS menu bar quick-saver</td>
                <td>Free and paid plans</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>
                  <strong>Pinboard</strong>
                </td>
                <td>Keyword and tag search</td>
                <td>Minimal tag-based saving</td>
                <td>Link metadata only</td>
                <td>HTML/XML export; optional archive</td>
                <td>Minimalist text archive purists</td>
                <td>$22/yr to $35/yr</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 3: Retrieval -->
      <section id="retrieval-battery" class="index-section">
        <h2>Retrieval & semantic search</h2>
        <p>
          The core failure mode of link saving is retrieval failure. Exact keyword lookup works when you remember a title, URL, or tag. Conceptual recall is different: you remember an idea, but not the words on the saved page.
        </p>

        <div class="battery-split">
          <div class="battery-text">
            <h3>The Keyword Match Bottleneck</h3>
            <p>
              Traditional bookmark managers such as Raindrop.io, Anybox, and Pinboard index only page titles, user-entered tags, and static descriptions. When a user searched for <em>"database vacuum dead tuples"</em>, keyword search returned 0 results because the actual article title was <em>"PostgreSQL Internals: Understanding MVCC Under Heavy Write Loads"</em>.
            </p>
            <h3>When semantic search matters</h3>
            <p>
              On Pro, Marqly searches the meaning of saved pages, highlights, and transcripts. That makes it a better fit when your normal query sounds like “that article about database cleanup” rather than a remembered title. Raindrop and Pinboard remain strong when a carefully maintained tag system is the way you prefer to retrieve information.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="stat-card">
            <div class="stat-num">2 modes</div>
            <div class="stat-label">Keyword recall vs conceptual recall</div>
            <p class="stat-desc">Choose based on how you actually remember saved material.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 4: Filing effort -->
      <section id="filing-latency" class="index-section">
        <h2>Filing overhead & maintenance</h2>
        <p>
          “Tag fatigue” appears when every save asks you to choose a collection, normalize a tag, and maintain a taxonomy. Some users value that control; others stop filing once their library becomes busy.
        </p>

        <div class="metrics-grid">
          <div class="metric-box">
            <h4>Manual control</h4>
            <div class="metric-val">Folders</div>
            <p>Raindrop is the clearer choice for deliberate nested collections and visual filing.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="metric-box">
            <h4>Automated filing</h4>
            <div class="metric-val">AI</div>
            <p>Marqly Pro adds AI tags and summaries to each save.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="metric-box">
            <h4>Local ownership</h4>
            <div class="metric-val">Self-host</div>
            <p>Karakeep and Linkwarden trade convenience for infrastructure control.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p>
          Marqly Free covers saving, boards, highlights, and whole-library keyword search. AI auto-tagging, summaries, semantic search, and the AI Organizer are Pro features. If you prefer manual oversight, Raindrop.io provides a polished visual filing workflow with nested collection pickers.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 5: Multimedia -->
      <section id="multimedia" class="index-section">
        <h2>Multimedia & video capture</h2>
        <p>
          In 2027, research is inherently multimedia. Software developers, analysts, and students spend as much time watching conference talks, tutorials, and YouTube lectures as reading essays. We evaluated how each bookmark manager handles video content.
        </p>

        <div class="multimedia-compare">
          <div class="mm-card">
            <h3>Legacy Bookmark Managers</h3>
            <p>
              Tools like Raindrop, Linkwarden, and Pinboard treat YouTube URLs like any standard web page: they fetch the YouTube title, thumbnail image, and description snippet. The spoken audio and instructional content remain inaccessible unless you manually re-watch the video.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="mm-card highlight-card">
              <h3>Marqly YouTube AI Watch Card (Pro)</h3>
            <p>
              Marqly embeds directly into YouTube watch pages. It automatically extracts full timestamped transcripts, generates structured executive summaries with key takeaways, and lets you chat directly with the video content. The full transcript text becomes instantly searchable in your Marqly dashboard.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 6: Portability -->
      <section id="portability" class="index-section">
        <h2>Portability, export fidelity & vendor lock-in</h2>
        <p>
          Data portability is a critical benchmark category. A bookmark library represents years of curated knowledge; if a service shuts down, hikes prices, or changes policy, you must be able to leave without losing your tags, folders, or notes.
        </p>
        <p>
          Before committing, verify that a tool exposes a documented export containing the metadata you care about. Standard <strong>Netscape bookmark HTML</strong> is the safest baseline for links and folder structure; CSV or JSON can preserve richer fields when supported.
        </p>

        <div class="portability-callout">
          <h3>Auditing Your Existing Exports</h3>
          <p>
            If you are currently using Raindrop.io and considering an upgrade, you can inspect your export file using our free <a href="/tools/raindrop-export-analyzer">Raindrop Export Analyzer</a>. It audits your collections, broken URLs, and duplicate tags in seconds.
          </p>
          <p>
            When you are ready to switch, our detailed <a href="/migrate/raindrop">Raindrop Migration Guide</a> walks you through importing a Raindrop HTML export into Marqly. Plan around two documented limits rather than discovering them afterwards: folder structure flattens to at most two board levels, and imported items take the import date instead of their original save date. Auto-tagging an import is a Pro feature.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 8: Choosing by audience (new for the 2027 edition) -->
      <section id="by-audience" class="index-section">
        <h2>Choosing by audience</h2>
        <p>
          The matrix above answers "which tool is best" generically, and the honest answer is that the binding constraint is usually <em>who the library is for</em>. Three audiences have requirements that reorder this whole table, and each has a dedicated comparison written against its actual decision criteria.
        </p>
        <div class="framework-grid">
          <div class="framework-card">
            <h3>Teachers & educators</h3>
            <p>
              The decisive question is whether students can open a shared resource list with no account. Marqly's public boards do that free on every plan; Diigo is the only tool here with a published free plan for verified educators; and Marqly publishes no educator discount at all.
              <a href="/blog/best-bookmark-managers-for-teachers-2027">The teacher comparison →</a>
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="framework-card">
            <h3>Schools & departments</h3>
            <p>
              Institutional buying turns on seat minimums, one bill, admin roles, SSO/SCIM and what happens when a licence lapses. Marqly Teams needs three seats and has neither SSO nor SCIM — a hard blocker for some districts — but cancellation turns the workspace read-only and deletes nothing.
              <a href="/blog/best-bookmark-organizers-for-schools-2027">The schools comparison →</a>
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="framework-card">
            <h3>Students</h3>
            <p>
              Cost across a multi-year arc plus retrieval at citation time. Raindrop.io is unlimited on free; Marqly charges verified students $48 for the first year; Weava offers 70% off with an education email but carries real maintenance risk.
              <a href="/blog/best-bookmark-managers-for-students-2027">The student comparison →</a>
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p>
          Two adjacent questions come up often enough to link directly. If budget is the binding constraint, <a href="/blog/best-free-bookmark-managers-2027">the free-tier comparison</a> names what each free plan actually caps and which "free" tools are really demos. If you are rescuing an old archive rather than starting fresh, <a href="/blog/best-pocket-alternatives-2027">the Pocket alternatives guide</a> covers which export file works and what to do if you never exported. For the AI question specifically, <a href="/blog/best-ai-bookmark-manager-2027">the AI bookmark manager guide</a> publishes six tests that separate real semantic search from marketing.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 9: Individual Tool Profiles -->
      <section id="tool-profiles" class="index-section">
            <h2>Tool profiles by best fit</h2>
        <p>
          These profiles summarize who each product suits and the trade-offs visible in its documented feature set. Follow the comparison links for deeper, source-maintained detail.
        </p>

        <div class="tool-cards">
          <!-- Marqly Card -->
          <div class="tool-card featured-tool">
            <div class="tool-header">
              <div>
                <span class="tool-rank">Best for AI retrieval</span>
                <h3>Marqly</h3>
                <p class="tool-tagline">The definitive AI bookmark manager for high-velocity research and semantic retrieval.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="pros-cons-grid">
              <div class="pros-col">
                <h4>Key Advantages</h4>
                <ul>
                  <li>Semantic search on Pro retrieves links by conceptual memory.</li>
                  <li>AI auto-tagging and summaries on Pro reduce manual filing.</li>
                  <li>YouTube AI Watch Card on Pro adds synchronized transcripts, summaries, and chat.</li>
                  <li>Persistent multi-color web highlighting and save-as-PDF capabilities.</li>
                  <li>Free tier includes up to 100 bookmarks and whole-library keyword search.</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
              <div class="cons-col">
                <h4>Trade-offs</h4>
                <ul>
                  <li>Optimized for modern web workflows rather than retro text-only terminals.</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="tool-actions">
              <a href="https://app.marqly.com" class="btn btn--primary">Get started free →</a>
              <a href="/pricing" class="btn btn--outline">View Pricing</a>
              <a href="/compare/marqly-vs-raindrop" class="tool-link">Compare Marqly vs Raindrop →</a>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- Raindrop Card -->
          <div class="tool-card">
            <div class="tool-header">
              <div>
                <span class="tool-rank">Best for visual collections</span>
                <h3>Raindrop.io</h3>
                <p class="tool-tagline">A polished visual collection organizer with comprehensive cross-platform apps.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="pros-cons-grid">
              <div class="pros-col">
                <h4>Key Advantages</h4>
                <ul>
                  <li>Gorgeous visual view modes (moodboard, headlines, cards).</li>
                  <li>Deep nested collection hierarchies with custom icons.</li>
                  <li>Wide client coverage across macOS, Windows, Linux, iOS, and Android.</li>
                  <li>Permanent web archive snapshots on paid Pro tier.</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
              <div class="cons-col">
                <h4>Trade-offs</h4>
                <ul>
                  <li>Search relies on exact keywords; fails on conceptual or synonym queries.</li>
                  <li>Requires manual folder selection and tagging on every save.</li>
                  <li>No native YouTube transcript parsing or AI video chat.</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="tool-actions">
              <a href="/compare/marqly-vs-raindrop" class="btn btn--outline">Marqly vs Raindrop In-Depth</a>
              <a href="/alternatives/raindrop" class="tool-link">Top Raindrop Alternatives</a>
              <a href="/migrate/raindrop" class="tool-link">Migrate from Raindrop →</a>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- Readwise Reader Card -->
          <div class="tool-card">
            <div class="tool-header">
              <div>
                <span class="tool-rank">Best for intensive reading</span>
                <h3>Readwise Reader</h3>
